# Environment Configuration Rules

## Scope
- Applies to all code under `ctf/`
- Governs environment setup, deployment topology, and Clerk authentication for the single production environment
- Environment variables are a critical contract and cannot be renamed without explicit user consent

## Single Production Environment (Render)

No local dev. The v3 app runs as a **single production environment on Render**:
the `ctf-web` service plus `ctf-route-weather` (see `render.yaml`). The Formance
ledger, Infisical, and Unleash deliberately stay on Railway (cost decision
recorded in `render.yaml`); the Ollama Render service was removed 2026-06-14
(moved to a RunPod endpoint). There is no separate staging *deployment* —
splitting was constrained by Clerk's domain binding (one Clerk instance per
domain), and v3 ships to the single production domain.

- **Frontend + Backend**: one Next.js app (`ctf-web`) on Render.
- **Domain**: the production domain (e.g. `app.chargingthefuture.com`).
- **Clerk Identity**: one production Clerk instance bound to that domain.
- **Release gating**: feature flags (Unleash + OpenFeature), not separate
  environments — see the Feature Flags section below.

### Per-environment credentials for Stream and Formance (the exception)

Two third-party integrations still need a non-production credential set even
though there is only one deployment, and `demo-mode` selects it at runtime:

- **GetStream** — the Maker-tier quota is a hard ceiling (see
  [110-stream-maker-tier-rules.mdc](110-stream-maker-tier-rules.mdc)). A separate
  Stream app supplies `STREAM_API_KEY_STAGING` / `STREAM_API_SECRET_STAGING` so
  demo/recording traffic never burns the production quota.
- **Formance** — real financial data. A separate ledger book on the same Formance
  instance (`FORMANCE_LEDGER_STAGING`, e.g. `ctf-demo`) keeps demo transactions
  out of the production ledger (`FORMANCE_LEDGER` = `ctf-main`). API URL, token,
  and asset are shared because both books live on the one instance.

When `demo-mode` is ON, `resolveStreamCredentials()` (web Stream readers) and the
Formance config reader select the `*_STAGING` values; if a `*_STAGING` value is
missing they report "not configured" rather than falling back to production, so a
demo session can never touch the production Stream quota or the real ledger.

## Clerk Authentication Requirements

### One production Clerk instance (domain-bound)
Clerk authentication is strictly domain-bound: session cookies and redirect URIs
must match the exact domain, and Clerk keys cannot be shared across domains.
Because v3 ships to a single production domain, it uses **one** Clerk instance.
This domain binding is precisely why the app could not be split into separate
staging/prod deployments without a second domain and a second Clerk instance.

### Expected auth environment variables
The auth layer is provider-neutral (`lib/auth/provider-env.ts`); keys are not
Clerk-name-specific:
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_PUBLISHABLE_KEY`
- `AUTH_SECRET_KEY`
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_SIGN_IN_URL` — **Clerk's hosted Account Portal sign-in URL**
  (e.g. `https://accounts.<domain>/sign-in`), NOT `/sign-in` on the app's own
  domain. See "Hosted sign-in" below.
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_PROVIDER` (the single provider-name variable; defaults to `clerk` if unset)
- `CLERK_ENCRYPTION_KEY`

### Hosted sign-in (Clerk Account Portal)

Sign-in and sign-up run on **Clerk's own domains** (the hosted Account Portal —
`https://accounts.<domain>` in production, `https://<slug>.accounts.dev` for a
dev instance), not on a page rendered on the app's domain. The app redirects to
the portal and Clerk redirects back when the flow completes.

- `NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_SIGN_IN_URL` must therefore be the Account Portal URL. The
  in-app `/sign-in` route is only a catch-all that forwards to that URL, so
  pointing it back at the app's own host (`/sign-in` or
  `https://<app-domain>/sign-in`) makes it redirect to itself forever
  (`ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS`).
- If the value is missing, relative, or same-host, the app self-corrects by
  deriving the Account Portal from `NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_PUBLISHABLE_KEY`
  (`deriveAccountPortalOrigin` in `lib/auth/provider-env.ts`), and
  `scripts/check-auth-env.mjs` prints a warning. Set the variable explicitly so
  configuration matches behavior.

### Clerk Configuration Constraints
- Do not share a Clerk instance across domains.
- The Clerk instance's redirect URIs must exactly match the production domain.
- Session/auth failures often indicate a Clerk domain mismatch.

### Clerk session token claims (username + role)

`middleware.ts` reads the signed-in user's username and role from the Clerk
**session token claims** and forwards them as `x-ctf-username` / `x-ctf-user-role`.
Clerk does not include these by default, so the session token must be customized
(Clerk dashboard → Configure → Sessions → "Customize session token"):

```json
{ "username": "{{user.username}}", "metadata": "{{user.public_metadata}}" }
```

Roles live in the user's **public metadata** (e.g. `{ "role": "admin" }`). Without
this configuration the app falls back to an anonymous display name and no role
(no regression), but username-gated routes deny with `missing_username` and admin
checks fail. Approval is not in the token — it comes from the database unlock tier.

## Environment Variable Contract

### Infisical is the single source of truth
All secrets live in the self-hosted Infisical instance, in a single `production`
environment. There is no `.env.local.example` file and no per-environment prefix
scheme (`RAILWAY_*` / `VERCEL_*` are retired). Render injects the Infisical
`production` secrets into `ctf-web` at deploy time.

**Critical Rule**: Secret keys CANNOT be renamed, removed, or restructured
without explicit user consent. New keys are added in Infisical (and documented
here) only with the owner's approval.

### No new variant for a value that already exists — and no dual-source fallbacks (Critical — all agents)

Agents keep injecting a brand-new environment variable name for a value that already has one, then
wiring a dual-source fallback so the new name is preferred and the existing one is the backstop. This
is **not allowed** without the owner explicitly asking for it and saying yes.

- **Reuse the existing key. Do not invent a synonym.** If the production base URL is already
  available as the `NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL` secret, a workflow that needs it must read
  `${{ secrets.NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL }}` directly. Do **not** introduce a new `APP_BASE_URL` (or any
  other renamed variant) for the same value.
- **No fallback expressions that add a new name.** Do not write
  `${{ vars.NEW_NAME || secrets.EXISTING }}` in a workflow, or `process.env.NEW_NAME ?? process.env.EXISTING`
  in code, where `NEW_NAME` is a name you are inventing for a value that already exists under
  `EXISTING`. The `||`/`??` chain looks harmless but it quietly creates a third config knob for one
  value, and the owner then has to guess which of several names is actually read.
- **The legitimate exception (allowed):** one logical value that genuinely lives under *different
  names in genuinely different sources/environments*. For example the app URL is `APP_URL` in
  Infisical (the app runtime) and `NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL` in GitHub Actions secrets — those are two real
  stores, so a reader bridging exactly those two is fine. What is forbidden is adding *another* name
  on top of an existing one in the *same* store for the *same* value.
- **If you think a new variable or a fallback is genuinely warranted, ask first.** State the value, the
  name(s) it already has, and why a new one is needed; only add it after the owner says yes. This is the
  same approval bar as renaming a key (above).

### Stream and Formance staging keys
The single `production` Infisical environment additionally holds the demo/staging
credential set for the two integrations that need it:

- `STREAM_API_KEY`, `STREAM_API_SECRET` (production) and `STREAM_API_KEY_STAGING`,
  `STREAM_API_SECRET_STAGING` (the demo Stream app — separate per-app quota).
- `FORMANCE_API_URL`, `FORMANCE_LEDGER` (= `ctf-main`), `FORMANCE_API_TOKEN`,
  `FORMANCE_ASSET` (production) and `FORMANCE_LEDGER_STAGING` (= `ctf-demo`, the
  demo ledger book on the same Formance instance).

`demo-mode` selects between the two sets at request time; see the
per-environment-credentials note above.

### Web Push (VAPID) keys — Foundation instant-call ring

Web Push (the browser notification that wakes a provider's device when a member rings them on
Foundation — issue #808 task 5) needs three values:

- `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` — the public application-server key. NOT secret; it is served to the browser so
  it can create a push subscription.
- `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` — the matching private key. Server-only; never sent to a client, never logged.
- `VAPID_SUBJECT` — a contact identifier for the push services, either a `mailto:` address or an
  `https://` URL (e.g. `mailto:ops@chargingthefuture.example`).

The owner generates the pair once, locally, with `npx web-push generate-vapid-keys`, then stores all
three in Infisical `production` (and any preview environment that should deliver push). Do **not**
generate or commit these anywhere in the repo — this is an open-source repository.

When any of the three is unset, Web Push degrades gracefully: `resolveWebPushKeys` in
`ctf/packages/web/lib/notifications/push.ts` returns null, every send is a logged no-op (it never
throws), and the in-app incoming-call poll remains the fallback. So builds, tests, and the call flow
work with these unset; setting them only switches the out-of-app push on.

### Expo push access token — Foundation instant-call ring (Android native push)

Android native push (the Expo notification that wakes a provider's phone when a member rings them on
Foundation — issue #884) is the Android counterpart of the Web Push above. The send goes to Expo's
push API (`https://exp.host/--/api/v2/push/send`) and may need ONE optional secret:

- `EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN` — an Expo account access token. **Server-only**; never sent to a client, never
  logged. It is **optional**: it is required only when the Expo project has "Enhanced Security for
  Push Notifications" turned on. A project without that setting accepts unauthenticated sends, so an
  unset token is a valid configuration, not an error.

The owner generates it (in the Expo dashboard, under the account's access tokens) only if enhanced
push security is on, then stores it in Infisical `production`. Do **not** generate or commit it
anywhere in the repo — this is an open-source repository. There is no public key counterpart: unlike
VAPID, the device registers with an Expo push token fetched at runtime in the app, not from a
server-provided key.

When `EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN` is unset, the send degrades gracefully: `sendExpoPushToUser` in
`ctf/packages/web/lib/notifications/expo-push.ts` still posts to Expo without an auth header (which
works for projects that do not require it), never throws, and the in-app incoming-call poll remains
the fallback. So builds, tests, and the call flow work with it unset. Note the Android device side
also needs an EAS native build (the `expo-notifications` config plugin) — native push does not work
in Expo Go regardless of this env var.

### Contributions plugin: owner Signal contact

| Variable | Layer | Used by | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| `CONTRIBUTIONS_OWNER_SIGNAL_URL` | Server | web (Node) | The owner's Signal contact (a `signal.me` URL or phone number) shown to signed-in members on the contribution confirmation screen so they know where to send a gift-card code. |

- Managed in Infisical (the single source of truth), `production` environment.
- **Server-only.** It is deliberately **not** prefixed `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, so it never enters the
  client bundle; it is read server-side only (`lib/contributions/owner-signal-env.ts`) and surfaced
  to the browser only through the `GET /api/contributions/fundraiser` response field `ownerSignalUrl`.
- **Optional.** When unset or empty the API returns `ownerSignalUrl: null` and the confirmation
  screen falls back to the admin-editable `signal_instructions` copy, so the screen never breaks.
- **Never commit or log the value.** It holds personal contact info; do not print it in logs,
  errors, or job summaries.

### PeerProgramming plugin: single standing, always-open Cohort 1 mode

`PEER_PROGRAMMING_SINGLE_OPEN_COHORT` switches the PeerProgramming plugin between two cohort models.
It is read server-side by one resolver, `isPeerProgrammingSingleOpenCohortEnabled()` in
`ctf/packages/web/lib/peer-programming/constants.ts` — do not read this variable anywhere else.

**Now a fallback under an in-app admin setting.** The env flag is no longer the source of truth. The
effective mode is resolved by the async resolver `isSingleOpenCohortModeEnabled()` (and
`resolveSingleOpenCohortMode()`) in `ctf/packages/web/lib/peer-programming/repository.ts` with this
precedence: (a) the persisted admin setting
(`peer_programming_settings.single_open_cohort_enabled`) if it is set (non-null) → use it; (b) else
this env flag; (c) else the built-in default (ON). An admin flips the setting from the PeerProgramming
admin surface (`POST /api/peer-programming/admin/single-open-cohort`), which supersedes this env flag
without a redeploy; clearing the admin setting (null) reverts to the env flag, then the default. With
no admin setting and no env override, behavior is unchanged: ON.

- **Defaults ON (temporary low-population mode).** Unset or empty is treated as ON. Only the explicit
  values `0` or `false` (case-insensitive) turn it OFF. The default is ON on purpose: while there are
  not enough active members to fill weekly cohorts of five, everyone shares one standing, always-open
  Cohort 1 instead of being split into tiny one-person rooms.
- **When ON:** there is a single standing cohort (the one row with `is_standing = TRUE`, label `C1`,
  always open). Any active member who opens PeerProgramming auto-joins it, so they can post and not
  just listen. The weekly auto-split is paused: the cron and the admin "run weekly assignment" simply
  add the active members to the standing cohort instead of forming `C1`/`C2`/`C3`.
- **To restore the original weekly cohorting,** set `PEER_PROGRAMMING_SINGLE_OPEN_COHORT=0` (or
  `false`). With the flag OFF, the plugin behaves exactly as before this mode was added: members are
  sliced into week-scoped cohorts of five and `getMyCohort` resolves the current week's cohort.
- **Optional, no secret.** It is a plain on/off switch, holds nothing sensitive, and can be logged.

## Implementation Rules

### When Deploying
1. Verify the correct Clerk instance is active for the target environment
2. Confirm `NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL` matches the deployment domain
3. Test sign-in and sign-out flows before declaring deployment complete
4. Do not mix environment variables across deployments

### When Modifying Environment Variables
- Notify the user immediately
- Add the new key in Infisical with owner approval (there is no `.env.local.example` file —
  Infisical is the single source of truth)
- Document the purpose of new variables in this rule or a related configuration document in the
  same commit

### When Troubleshooting Auth Issues
1. Verify the Clerk instance matches the deployment domain
2. Check that `NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` is the correct one for the domain
3. Confirm redirect URIs in Clerk dashboard match the deployment domain (exact match)
4. Confirm the Render service environment synced from Infisical, and remember `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`
   values are baked into the image at build time (`build-images.yml`) — changing them needs a
   rebuild, not just a redeploy
5. Check browser console for Clerk-specific error messages

## Reserved Username Prefixes (Skills Hunt + Directory co-change)

Skills Hunt auto-generates `community-<6hex>` handles for unclaimed Directory profiles (`directory_profiles.unclaimed_handle`). Allowing a Clerk-managed user to claim a username starting with `community-` would let them impersonate a community-generated profile.

**Server-side defense:** `lib/auth/username-policy.ts` rejects submissions from any user whose Clerk `username` starts with a reserved prefix (returns `SKILLS_HUNT_RESERVED_USERNAME` 403).

**Clerk dashboard configuration (REQUIRED on the production Clerk instance):**

1. Open the Clerk dashboard for the instance.
2. Go to **User & authentication → Username**.
3. Add `community-` to the **disallowed usernames** / blocklist as a prefix pattern.
4. If your Clerk plan only exposes regex-based blocking, use `^community-`.
5. Save and re-test with a sign-up attempt using `community-test` — Clerk should reject before reaching the server.

The server-side check is the source of truth; the Clerk dashboard layer is defense-in-depth so users get a clear error at signup time instead of after their first submission.

**When this list changes:**
- Update `SKILLS_HUNT_RESERVED_USERNAME_PREFIXES` in `lib/auth/username-policy.ts`.
- Update the Clerk dashboard on the production Clerk instance.
- Document the new prefix in this rule.

## Feature Flags (Unleash + OpenFeature)

Release gating and progressive user access use **Unleash** behind an **OpenFeature** client
(vendor-neutral interface). Runtime hosting and secret sync are separate concerns: the **Unleash
server is self-hosted on Railway**, while the app's **environment variables sync from Infisical to
Render** (where `ctf-web` runs). Single production environment: code ships with flags OFF by default;
flags are toggled per-agent-branch/PR or by rollout. See issues #103 (epic), #101 (unlock-as-flag),
#102 (public-screen gating + demo-safe data).

### Required environment variables (synced via Infisical → Render Sync)

| Variable | Layer | Used by | Pairs with |
|---|---|---|---|
| `UNLEASH_API_URL` | Both | All services | Base URL of the Unleash instance. Server SDK uses the **Client API** (`/api/`); the frontend client uses the **Frontend API** (`/api/frontend`). |
| `UNLEASH_API_TOKEN_BACKEND` | Server | web (Node), agents | Server-side `unleash-client` SDK (Client API). **Never exposed to the browser.** |
| `UNLEASH_API_TOKEN_FRONTEND` | Client | mobile (Expo), browser | Client-side reads via the Unleash Frontend API. |

Optional: `UNLEASH_APP_NAME` (default `ctf-web`), `UNLEASH_ENVIRONMENT`.

When `UNLEASH_API_URL` + `UNLEASH_API_TOKEN_BACKEND` are absent (local/CI), the server flag client
falls back to OpenFeature's default provider, which returns each call's supplied default — i.e. flags
are safely OFF. This is the SDK's defined unconfigured behavior, not a placeholder.

### Flag-key naming convention (source of truth: `ctf/packages/shared/src/feature-flags/keys.ts`)

- `feature-{pluginSlug}-{featureName}` — user-facing feature gating (e.g. `feature-unlock-quora-onboarding`).
- `release-{agent}-{fixName}` — release/rollout gating for a specific change.
- System flags (cross-cutting): `demo-mode`, `public-surface`.

### Targeting strategy

- Per-user targeting and sticky percentage rollout key off the OpenFeature `targetingKey`
  (mapped to the Unleash `userId`); use the caller's Clerk user id.
- Segment/role/region targeting passes through `FeatureFlagContext.attributes` → Unleash `properties`.
- Unleash logs every toggle (audit trail); flag changes are an operational step separate from deploys.

## Precedence
This rule takes precedence over all other environment and deployment rules.
Breaking this rule will directly cause Clerk authentication to fail and prevent users from signing in.

## Summary Table

| Aspect | Production (Render) |
|--------|---------------------|
| Frontend Host | Render (`ctf-web`) |
| Backend Host | Render (`ctf-web`) |
| Domain | Custom production domain |
| Clerk Instance | One, bound to the production domain |
| Secrets | Infisical `production` (single source of truth) |
| Per-env credentials | Stream + Formance `*_STAGING` keys, selected by `demo-mode` |
| Release gating | Feature flags (Unleash + OpenFeature), not environments |
